.TH xmail "" "Command"
.SH NAME
xmail \- secret computer mail
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBxmail \fIuser ...\fR
.br
\fBxmail [\-n | \-x | \-y]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
xmail - secret computer mail
Usage:	xmail user ...
	xmail [ -nxy ]
If 'user' is present, send each a message encrypted with the user's public key.
Mail message ends with EOF or a line containing only '.'.
Otherwise, read encoded mail.
Options:
	-n	Discard mail
	-x 	Save plain text in $HOME/mbox
	-y 	Save mail in encrypted form in $HOME/xmbox
.HE
.B xmail
sends and reads mail that is encrypted so that only its intended
recipient can read it, using per-user public keys kept under
.BR /usr/spool/pubkey .
.PP
If one or more
.I user
arguments are given,
.B xmail
checks each name against
.BR /usr/spool/pubkey ,
printing
.B "user is not enrolled."
for any name with no public-key file there and excluding it from the
send list; if none of the named recipients is enrolled,
.B xmail
exits without sending anything.
It builds a header (a
.B From
line naming the sender and the time, plus a
.B "(cc: ...)"
line listing the other recipients if there is more than one), reads
the message from the standard input up to end of file or a line
containing only
.BR . ,
and, for each valid recipient, pipes the header and message through the
filter
.B /bin/xencode
(run as a child process, using that recipient's public key) and appends
the encrypted result to
.B xmailbox
in the recipient's home directory.
If the recipient already has a regular mailbox, a short notice that
mail is waiting is also appended there.
.PP
With no
.I user
argument,
.B xmail
instead reads the caller's own accumulated secret mail: it pipes
.B $HOME/xmailbox
through the filter
.B /bin/xdecode
(which prompts for a passphrase), copies the decrypted text to a
private, unlinked temporary file under
.BR /tmp ,
and prints it, then asks
.BR "Save ?" .
Answering
.B x
saves the decrypted text, followed by whatever
.B $HOME/mbox
already held, back into
.BR $HOME/mbox ;
answering
.B y
does the same but keeps the mail encrypted and saves it into
.BR $HOME/xmbox ;
answering
.B n
discards it; any other answer leaves
.B $HOME/xmailbox
untouched, so the same mail is read again next time.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Discard the accumulated secret mail without displaying the "Save ?"
prompt.
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Save the decrypted mail into
.B $HOME/mbox
without prompting.
.IP \fB\-y\fR
Save the still-encrypted mail into
.B $HOME/xmbox
without prompting.
.PP
The
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-x ,
and
.B \-y
options apply only when reading the caller's own mail (no
.I user
argument); they may not be combined with each other.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/usr/spool/pubkey/\fIuser\fR	each user's public key
\fB$HOME/xmailbox\fR	incoming encrypted mail
\fB$HOME/mbox\fR	saved, decrypted mail
\fB$HOME/xmbox\fR	saved, still-encrypted mail
\fB/bin/xencode\fR	encryption filter
\fB/bin/xdecode\fR	decryption filter
\fB/tmp/xm*\fR	private temporary file for decrypted text
.DE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B xmail
prints
.B "user is not enrolled."
for each named recipient with no public-key file, and exits without
sending if none of the recipients is enrolled;
.B "Cannot send to user"
if a recipient's home directory cannot be found;
.B "No xmail"
if there is no secret mail waiting to be read; and
.B "Cannot save clear text, no action taken"
for
.B \-x
if there is no mail to decrypt.
.SH NOTES
.B xmail
must be installed setuid to root, since only root may read every
user's entry under the protected directory
.BR /usr/spool/pubkey ;
the source comments that this is "regarded as a failing."
.PP
The filters
.B /bin/xdecode
and
.BR /bin/xencode ,
and the
.B enroll
command that creates a user's public-key file, are referenced by
.B xmail
but are not present in this source tree.
